---
/**
 * ComparisonCardsBlock — the `comparison_cards` layout: this against that.
 *
 * Extracted from the `compare` band of
 * src/pages/cosmetic-dentistry/clear-aligners.astro:290-336, not designed anew.
 * The markup below is that band's markup — same elements, same order, same
 * class names — because the phase is verified by diffing built HTML and a
 * different wrapper changes the page the moment it is backfilled
 * (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 3.3).
 *
 * The same `.compare` / `.compare-card` shell is inline on nine templates
 * today (both cosmetic and implant families), so this block lands ready-made
 * on the whole Phase 3 list.
 *
 * ── The CTA row: field-driven since the census batch; the fallback IS the
 *    contract ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The band ends with a `.cta-row`: two Buttons and a `.cta-note`. The layout
 * now carries the seven cta fields (`cta_label`…`cta_note`, the
 * block_cta_fields factory in vs-content-model.php) because the row is page
 * copy the client must be able to reword. But eleven back-filled routes were
 * verified byte-for-byte against a LITERAL row — Free Virtual Consult →
 * #consult hover "Get a Video"; Read FAQ → #faq hover "See Answers"; note
 * "No commitment · Personal video reply" — and every one of their saved rows
 * has all seven fields blank. So while BOTH labels are blank this component
 * draws exactly that literal row: never no row, never a partial one. One
 * non-blank label switches the WHOLE row to the fields — the literal pair
 * never mixes with an edited one, because half-and-half is the "both sides
 * assumed the other had it" failure mode this census batch exists to close.
 *
 * HREF POLICY: a cta href field stores an in-page anchor ("#consult"), a site
 * path ("/smile-gallery/"), or one of the tokens `book` | `phone` | `map`,
 * resolved below from src/data/contact.ts — never a pasted booking/tel/maps
 * URL, which goes stale the day the practice changes numbers or booking
 * vendors. Hover labels: a blank `cta_hover*` falls back to the
 * per-destination table; an unknown destination falls back to the visible
 * label (Button's own `hoverLabel ?? slot`), so a typo'd token degrades to a
 * working button instead of a wrong promise.
 *
 * PHASE 3 CAVEAT, still true with fields: `smile-makeover.astro:288` and
 * `full-mouth-rehabilitation.astro:233` reuse this same `.compare` shell for
 * their `options` band and draw NO cta-row, and blank fields here mean the
 * FALLBACK row, not no row. The fix remains a `show_cta` boolean on the PHP
 * layout, read here — not a heuristic on the anchor, which an editor can
 * rename.
 *
 * `callout_heading` + `callout_body` draw the `.safety-callout` aside that sits
 * between the cards and the CTA row on teeth-whitening.astro:495-509. It is a
 * field pair rather than a page-specific band because the copy it carries — the
 * pro-vs-DIY safety argument — is the same argument the cards above it make,
 * which is why that page put it inside the compare band instead of giving it a
 * section. Both fields are blank on the two pages this block renders today and
 * blank draws no aside at all, so adding them changes nothing until a page is
 * backfilled with them.
 *
 * `altColumns` + `altCards` draw the `.alternatives-grid` of `.config-card`s
 * that follows the comparison on all-on-4-single-arch.astro:611-620 and
 * full-mouth-dental-implants.astro:577-586, and that OPENS bone-grafting's
 * `#types` at bone-grafting.astro:527-536. A SECOND grid beside `tiers`, never a
 * replacement for it: `tiers` draws the wide `.compare-card`s with their bullet
 * lists, these are the smaller cards under them, and folding them into `tiers`
 * would give them bullets they do not have and an `<h3>` where the markup has
 * `<h4>`.
 *
 * WHERE IT SITS, AND WHY IT IS NOT INSIDE THE ASIDE. On the two `#compare`
 * pages the grid's sub-head is a `.compare-sub-head` — an eyebrow, an `<h3>` and
 * a paragraph — and the `<h3>` and paragraph are exactly what `calloutHeading` /
 * `calloutBody` already carry, which is why the contract selects `altCards`
 * immediately after `callout_body`. So the grid is drawn immediately after the
 * `.safety-callout` aside and before the CTA row, in that DOM order.
 *
 * It is a SIBLING of the aside rather than a child of it, and that is a
 * deliberate departure from the batch brief's wording, because nesting has two
 * concrete failure modes:
 *   1. `.safety-callout` is `max-width: 920px` with `36px 40px` of padding
 *      (teeth-whitening.css:425-439, restated below). The grid is full
 *      `.wrap` width — 1240px less 40px gutters — on all three source pages, so
 *      nesting it would visibly narrow every card and paint the row cream.
 *   2. bone-grafting's `#types` grid has NO sub-head at all (its section head
 *      does that job), so `calloutHeading`/`calloutBody` are blank there and
 *      `hasCallout` is false. A grid nested inside the aside would render
 *      nothing at all on that page — the exact loss this batch exists to close.
 * As a sibling, all three pages come out right with one code path: aside then
 * grid where there is a sub-head, grid alone where there is not.
 *
 * ADDITIVE. No row saved before this batch holds an `altCards` row, so the list
 * is empty and the `.alternatives-grid` WRAPPER IS INSIDE THE LENGTH TEST, not
 * around it — an empty grid div is a visible gap on eleven bands nobody edited.
 *
 * BOTH GAPS THE PREVIOUS REVISION RECORDED HERE ARE NOW CLOSED, by the census
 * batch's contract rather than by reinterpreting an existing field:
 *
 * `glossaryEyebrow`/`glossaryHeading`/`glossaryBody` + the `glossary` repeater
 * carry bone-grafting `#types`'s second shape below the grid — the
 * `.materials-block`: its own eyebrow / `<h3>` / `<p>` head beside a
 * `<dl class="materials-list">` of `<dt><span class="tag"><span class="label">`
 * over `<dd>` rows (bone-grafting.astro:548-565). Its own head trio and its
 * own repeater rather than a third reading of the callout pair, because that
 * band needs the callout pair AND this head at once, and `altCards` is spent
 * on the same band's `.alternatives-grid`. Drawn AFTER the grid and before
 * the CTA row — the source page's DOM order. `glossary` mints
 * PageFieldsBlocksGlossary; checked by enumeration in the PHP, claimed
 * nowhere else.
 *
 * `calloutEyebrow` closes the `.compare-sub-head` EYEBROW gap
 * ("Alternatives", all-on-4-single-arch.astro:607 and
 * full-mouth-dental-implants.astro:573): it opens the `.safety-callout`
 * aside, above the `<h3>` the callout pair already carries. The aside never
 * had one, so blank draws no element and every saved aside is unchanged.
 *
 * `bullets` renders the `<ul>` variant the implant pages use
 * (implant-dentistry.css:734-762). The pilot's three cards have no bullets, so
 * on the pilot this branch never fires — it is here so Phase 3 does not have to
 * reopen the component.
 *
 * `body2` (PHP `body_2`) is a tier's closing paragraph AFTER the bullets:
 * smile-makeover's featured veneers card runs body, bullet list, then a second
 * full paragraph (smile-makeover.astro:489-492), and one body slot dropped its
 * 26 words. HTML-bearing — same treatment as media_split's `body_2` — and
 * emitted flushed against the bullet expression so a card saved without one is
 * byte-identical.
 *
 * NOTHING HERE THROWS. An editor triggers deploys and never sees the output
 * (cms/mu-plugins/vs-deploy.php:142-158), so every field is read as if it might
 * be missing, blank or the wrong type. Same rule as PageBlocks.astro.
 *
 * Spec: docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 1.2, 1.3 (Wave A), 2.5, 3.3.
 */
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import { resolveCtaHref, resolveCtaHover } from "./cta";

/**
 * The five bands, mapped to the modifier this block writes on its own section.
 *
 * Read this map, not an ancestor class. `.section.alt` is charcoal green on
 * clear-aligners.css:240 and cream on teeth-whitening.css:237 — Fact 4 — so a
 * block that inherits its band is wrong on at least one page the moment a
 * section is reordered, and the eyebrow rules below would paint themselves the
 * colour of their own background (R2).
 *
 * `alt` and `dark` are kept as the modifier *names* for charcoal and pale sage
 * because the acceptance test for this phase is that the block's markup matches
 * the band it replaces, class attribute included. They no longer mean what the
 * page sheets mean by them: the meaning is the four rules in <style> below,
 * which this block carries onto any page it is dropped on.
 *
 * `paper` deliberately maps to no modifier at all — the pilot emits
 * `class="section"` for a paper band, so adding one would be the diff.
 */
const BAND_MODIFIER: Record<string, string | null> = {
  paper: null,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

/** One line of a card's bullet list. ACF has no list-of-strings, so it is a
 *  repeater with `lead` (the bolded lead-in, trailing em dash stored in the
 *  value) and `item` sub-fields (vs-content-model.php). */
interface BulletRow {
  lead?: string | null;
  item?: string | null;
}

interface CardRow {
  tag?: string | null;
  title?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
  bullets?: BulletRow[] | null;
  /** The paragraph AFTER the bullets — see the header comment. */
  body2?: string | null;
  ribbon?: string | null;
  featured?: boolean | null;
}

/**
 * One card of the `.alternatives-grid` under the comparison.
 *
 * Three pieces of copy and no more. Read off the markup rather than the class
 * names: `.config-card` is `<span class="tag">` + `<h4>` + `<p>` identically on
 * all-on-4-single-arch.astro:614-618, full-mouth-dental-implants.astro:580-584
 * and bone-grafting.astro:530-534. No `href`, no `ribbon`, no `featured` —
 * nothing in any of those grids is a link or carries a flash.
 *
 * `body` is optional because the twin shape on the media_split side
 * (sinus-lift's `.cause-card`) has no `<p>` at all, and a blank body must draw
 * NO paragraph rather than an empty one.
 */
interface AltCardRow {
  tag?: string | null;
  title?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
}

interface Props {
  /** The DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. Absent when blank. */
  id?: string;
  /** Passed by PageBlocks alongside `id`; unused here, accepted so the spread
   *  of the whole block row does not land in the rest props of a typed
   *  component that never declared it. */
  anchor?: string;
  band?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>` and nothing else (Fact 3), so it is rendered set:html. */
  heading?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
  /** Named `tiers` in PHP, not `cards`: card_grid.cards already mints
   *  PageFieldsBlocksCards, and a second repeater of that name merges into it. */
  tiers?: CardRow[] | null;
  /**
   * The small label the `.safety-callout` aside opens with, above the `<h3>` —
   * the `.compare-sub-head` eyebrow ("Alternatives") on both `#compare` pages
   * (all-on-4-single-arch.astro:607, full-mouth-dental-implants.astro:573).
   * Blank draws no element: the aside never had one before this batch.
   */
  calloutEyebrow?: string | null;
  /**
   * The `.safety-callout` aside that sits between the cards and the CTA row —
   * its `<h3>`. Reproduces teeth-whitening.astro:495-509, which hosts the "Is
   * Professional Teeth Whitening Safe" copy inside the compare band rather than
   * inventing a section for it. Blank on the pilot, and blank must draw nothing.
   */
  calloutHeading?: string | null;
  /**
   * The aside's prose. HTML-bearing on purpose: the real copy is two paragraphs
   * and the corpus puts `<a class="vs-link">` and `<em>` inside this kind of
   * body, so it is rendered set:html rather than as text. See the paragraph
   * split below for how two paragraphs survive a plain textarea.
   */
  calloutBody?: string | null;
  /**
   * How many `altCards` sit across a row on a desktop screen: "2", "3" or "4".
   *
   * NULL MEANS THREE, and null is what every one of the eleven back-filled
   * routes returns — ACF writes its "3" default only when an editor ADDS a row,
   * never onto rows saved before the field existed. A component that read null
   * as "unset" and fell back to one column would collapse a grid that has
   * always been three across on all-on-4-single-arch.css:530-531 and
   * bone-grafting.css:530-531. Two is a real choice, not a guess:
   * full-mouth-dental-implants.css:537-541 overrides to two because that page
   * has two alternatives.
   */
  altColumns?: string | null;
  /**
   * The smaller cards under the comparison — the alternatives, the material
   * sources, the other options. Named `altCards`, not `subCards`, because a
   * repeater's GraphQL type is its field name appended to the parent's prefix
   * and the layout contributes nothing: media_split's grid already mints
   * PageFieldsBlocksSubCards, and a second repeater of that name would merge
   * into it and silently stop one side's sub-fields being queryable.
   */
  altCards?: (AltCardRow | null)[] | null;
  /**
   * The `.materials-block` head, drawn AFTER the alternatives grid — see the
   * header comment for why this is its own trio and not the callout pair read
   * a second time. All three blank plus an empty `glossary` draws nothing.
   */
  glossaryEyebrow?: string | null;
  glossaryHeading?: string | null;
  glossaryBody?: string | null;
  /**
   * The `<dl class="materials-list">` rows. The same {tag, title, body} triple
   * as `AltCardRow` — reused on purpose, because the two shapes carry the same
   * three pieces of copy and diverge only in the wrapper that draws them — but
   * a separate PHP repeater (`glossary`, minting PageFieldsBlocksGlossary):
   * bone-grafting's `#types` needs BOTH at once, so one repeater cannot serve.
   */
  glossary?: (AltCardRow | null)[] | null;
  /**
   * The seven CTA fields — see the header comment for the fallback contract
   * and the href policy. All seven are blank on every row saved before this
   * batch, which is exactly the state that draws the literal row.
   */
  ctaLabel?: string | null;
  ctaHref?: string | null;
  ctaHover?: string | null;
  ctaLabel2?: string | null;
  ctaHref2?: string | null;
  ctaHover2?: string | null;
  ctaNote?: string | null;
}

const {
  id,
  band,
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  tiers,
  calloutEyebrow,
  calloutHeading,
  calloutBody,
  altColumns,
  altCards,
  glossaryEyebrow,
  glossaryHeading,
  glossaryBody,
  glossary,
  ctaLabel,
  ctaHref,
  ctaHover,
  ctaLabel2,
  ctaHref2,
  ctaHover2,
  ctaNote,
} = Astro.props;

// `?? null` rather than a default value: an unknown band from a future PHP
// choice must fall through to the paper shell rather than emit a class with no
// rule behind it.
const bandKey = band ?? "paper";
const bandModifier = BAND_MODIFIER[bandKey] ?? null;

/** A solid button on a dark band has to be the light variant, not the sage one.
 *  Same test as FaqBlock.astro:148, so the two bands agree on any page. */
const isDarkBand = bandKey === "charcoal" || bandKey === "sage";

const cardRows = Array.isArray(tiers) ? tiers : [];

/** Trim once, here, so every `{x && …}` guard below is a test for real content
 *  rather than for a field an editor left as a single space. */
const text = (value: unknown): string =>
  typeof value === "string" ? value.trim() : "";

const eyebrowText = text(eyebrow);
const headingHtml = text(heading);
const bodyText = text(body);

/* ── The safety callout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Both halves are new and both default to empty, so everything below has to
   collapse to nothing at all — not to an empty <aside>, not to a bare <h3>.
   The two live branch pages render this band today with both fields blank and
   the phase is verified by diffing built HTML, so a wrapper that survives an
   empty field is a failed phase (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 3.3). */
const calloutEyebrowText = text(calloutEyebrow);
const calloutHeadingText = text(calloutHeading);
const calloutBodyHtml = text(calloutBody);

/* The field is a textarea, so the expected value is inline markup and the `<p>`
   elements below are this component's. Same guard as MediaSplitBlock's `body_2`
   (MediaSplitBlock.astro:243-251): if wp-admin ever converts this to a wysiwyg,
   ACF's `wpautop` returns `<p>…</p>` already wrapped, and nesting that inside
   another `<p>` produces invalid markup whose parser recovery leaves a stray
   empty paragraph in the band. */
const calloutBodyIsBlock = /^<(p|div|ul|ol|blockquote)[\s>]/i.test(calloutBodyHtml);

/* The copy this replaces is TWO paragraphs, and `.safety-callout p + p` exists
   only to space them. Splitting on the blank line an editor already types is
   what lets a plain textarea reproduce that markup; without it the callout
   collapses to one paragraph, the `p + p` rule never fires, and the diff shows
   a missing element. Blank-line-separated, not newline-separated: a soft wrap
   inside a sentence must not become a paragraph break. */
const calloutParagraphs = calloutBodyIsBlock
  ? []
  : calloutBodyHtml
      .split(/\n\s*\n/)
      .map((part) => part.trim())
      .filter((part) => part !== "");

const hasCallout =
  calloutEyebrowText !== "" ||
  calloutHeadingText !== "" ||
  calloutBodyHtml !== "";

/* Derived from the anchor rather than hard-coded, because PageBlocks has
   already deduplicated the anchor (PageBlocks.astro:64-85) and two
   comparison_cards rows on one page would otherwise both emit
   id="safety-callout-h3" — a duplicate id that sends the accessible name of
   the second aside to the first one's heading. Nothing links to this value:
   its only consumer is the `aria-labelledby` on the element that owns it.
   `undefined` when there is no heading, so the aside never points at an id
   that was never written. */
const calloutHeadingId =
  calloutHeadingText === ""
    ? undefined
    : id
      ? `${id}-safety-callout`
      : "safety-callout-h3";

/* ── The alternatives grid ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Trimmed and emptied-out here so the single length test below is a test for
   real cards. A row an editor added and then left entirely blank must not draw
   an empty `.config-card`, and — more to the point — must not be the one row
   that makes `.alternatives-grid` render on a band that has no cards. */
const altCardRows = (Array.isArray(altCards) ? altCards : [])
  .map((row) => ({
    tag: text(row?.tag),
    title: text(row?.title),
    body: text(row?.body),
  }))
  .filter((row) => row.tag !== "" || row.title !== "" || row.body !== "");

/* A class rather than an inline `grid-template-columns`, because the count has
   to change again at the mobile breakpoint and an inline style would outrank
   the media query. Same three choices the PHP select offers
   (vs-content-model.php:2337-2352). */
const ALT_COLUMN_CLASS: Record<string, string> = {
  "2": "cols-2",
  "3": "cols-3",
  "4": "cols-4",
};

/* `?? "cols-3"` is the null case spelled out, not a defensive shrug: see
   `altColumns` above. An unrecognised future choice lands here too, which is
   the right place for it — three across is the shape four of the five source
   grids draw. */
const altColumnClass = ALT_COLUMN_CLASS[text(altColumns)] ?? "cols-3";

/* ── The materials glossary ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Same trim-and-drop discipline as `altCardRows` above, and for the same
   reason: one blank-but-saved row must not be the thing that makes the whole
   `.materials-block` render on a band that never had one. */
const glossaryRows = (Array.isArray(glossary) ? glossary : [])
  .map((row) => ({
    tag: text(row?.tag),
    title: text(row?.title),
    body: text(row?.body),
  }))
  .filter((row) => row.tag !== "" || row.title !== "" || row.body !== "");

const glossaryEyebrowText = text(glossaryEyebrow);
const glossaryHeadingText = text(glossaryHeading);
const glossaryBodyText = text(glossaryBody);

const hasGlossaryHead =
  glossaryEyebrowText !== "" ||
  glossaryHeadingText !== "" ||
  glossaryBodyText !== "";

/* Head OR rows, not head AND rows: an editor who has typed the heading but not
   yet added rows must see the heading on the page — a shape that silently
   requires both halves before showing either is the bone-grafting `#what`
   failure (55 words lost to a pair each side assumed the other rendered). */
const hasGlossary = hasGlossaryHead || glossaryRows.length > 0;

/* ── The CTA row ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Field-driven with the literal fallback — the full contract is in the header
   comment. Everything below computes three values (button one, button two, the
   note) so the template has a single rendering path: the fallback is DATA, not
   a second copy of the markup that could drift from the first.

   In the template the two button expressions sit on their OWN lines, breaking
   with the `}{` flush idiom used everywhere else in this file: in fallback
   mode both are truthy, and the newline-and-indent text node between them is
   exactly the text node the old literal template carried between its two
   <Button>s — flushing them would DELETE a byte the eleven verified routes
   render today. The flush idiom exists to keep an EMPTY expression from
   adding bytes; here the expressions are never both empty. */

/* Token map, hover table and both resolvers now live in ./cta.ts — one copy
   for every block that draws a CTA row. The literal-fallback logic below is
   this block's own and stays here. */

const ctaLabelText = text(ctaLabel);
const ctaLabel2Text = text(ctaLabel2);

/* ONE label switches the whole row to the fields (never literal + field mixed);
   BOTH blank is the literal row, byte-for-byte — invariant 2 of the census
   batch, eleven live routes depend on it. */
const ctaIsFieldDriven = ctaLabelText !== "" || ctaLabel2Text !== "";

interface CtaButton {
  label: string;
  href: string;
  hover: string | undefined;
}

const ctaOne: CtaButton | null = ctaIsFieldDriven
  ? ctaLabelText === ""
    ? null
    : {
        label: ctaLabelText,
        href: resolveCtaHref(text(ctaHref)),
        hover: resolveCtaHover(text(ctaHref), text(ctaHover)),
      }
  : { label: "Free Virtual Consult", href: "#consult", hover: "Get a Video" };

const ctaTwo: CtaButton | null = ctaIsFieldDriven
  ? ctaLabel2Text === ""
    ? null
    : {
        label: ctaLabel2Text,
        href: resolveCtaHref(text(ctaHref2)),
        hover: resolveCtaHover(text(ctaHref2), text(ctaHover2)),
      }
  : { label: "Read FAQ", href: "#faq", hover: "See Answers" };

/* In field mode a blank note draws no note — a reworded pair of buttons does
   not have to carry a caption. In fallback mode the note is one third of the
   literal row and always draws. */
const ctaNoteText = ctaIsFieldDriven
  ? text(ctaNote)
  : "No commitment · Personal video reply";
---

<section class:list={["section", bandModifier]} id={id} data-vs-reveal-batch>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      {eyebrowText && <span class="eyebrow">{eyebrowText}</span>}
      {headingHtml && <h2><Fragment set:html={headingHtml} /></h2>}
      {bodyText && <p>{bodyText}</p>}
    </div>

    <div class="compare">
      {
        cardRows.map((card) => {
          // `lead` is the bolded lead-in and carries its own trailing em dash
          // ("Adjacent teeth —"), because that dash sits INSIDE the <b> in every
          // template this reproduces. A row with no lead draws no <b> and no
          // leading space, so a list saved before `lead` existed is unchanged.
          const bullets = (Array.isArray(card?.bullets) ? card.bullets : [])
            .map((row) => ({ lead: text(row?.lead), item: text(row?.item) }))
            .filter((row) => row.lead !== "" || row.item !== "");

          const tag = text(card?.tag);
          const title = text(card?.title);
          const cardBody = text(card?.body);
          const cardBody2 = text(card?.body2);
          const ribbon = text(card?.ribbon);

          return (
            <article class:list={["compare-card", card?.featured && "featured"]}>
              {ribbon && <span class="ribbon">{ribbon}</span>}
              {tag && <span class="tag">{tag}</span>}
              {title && <h3>{title}</h3>}
              {cardBody && <p>{cardBody}</p>}
              {bullets.length > 0 && (
                <ul class="compare-list">
                  {bullets.map((row) => (
                    <li>{row.lead === "" ? row.item : (<Fragment><b>{row.lead}</b> {row.item}</Fragment>)}</li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              )}{/* `}{` flushed — same idiom as the altCards expression below:
                  a line of its own would put a text node inside every card
                  that has no body2, which is all of them but one. set:html for
                  the same reason as calloutBody: this is prose that may carry
                  an <em> mid-sentence. */
              cardBody2 && <p set:html={cardBody2} />}
            </article>
          );
        })
      }
    </div>

    {
      hasCallout && (
        <aside class="safety-callout" aria-labelledby={calloutHeadingId}>
          {/* Flushed against the heading expression: teeth-whitening's saved
              aside has no eyebrow, and an expression on its own line would add
              a text node inside an aside that is diff-verified today. */
          calloutEyebrowText && (
            <span class="eyebrow">{calloutEyebrowText}</span>
          )}{calloutHeadingText && <h3 id={calloutHeadingId}>{calloutHeadingText}</h3>}
          {calloutBodyIsBlock ? (
            <Fragment set:html={calloutBodyHtml} />
          ) : (
            calloutParagraphs.map((para) => <p set:html={para} />)
          )}
        </aside>
      )
    }{/* `}{` with nothing between, deliberately — the same idiom as
        MediaSplitBlock.astro:430,476,508. An expression on its own line carries
        a newline-and-indent TEXT NODE either side, and that survives into the
        built HTML as a rendered space even when the condition is false. Eleven
        routes are already back-filled against this layout and all eleven have an
        empty `altCards`, so a line of its own here would move `</aside> <div
        class="cta-row">` to `</aside>  <div class="cta-row">` on every one of
        them and fail the byte-for-byte diff. Flushed against the callout's
        closing brace, the pre-existing text node between the two just moves
        behind this expression and the empty case is unchanged.

        THE WRAPPER IS INSIDE THE TEST, not around it: `altCardRows.length > 0`
        gates the `<div class="alternatives-grid">` itself, so a band with no
        cards emits no empty grid div. */
      altCardRows.length > 0 && (
        <div class:list={["alternatives-grid", altColumnClass]}>
          {altCardRows.map((card) => (
            <article class="config-card">
              {card.tag && <span class="tag">{card.tag}</span>}
              {card.title && <h4>{card.title}</h4>}
              {card.body && <p>{card.body}</p>}
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      )
    }{/* `}{` flushed, same reasoning as above — twelve saved bands have no
        glossary and the text node a free-standing line adds would fail their
        byte-for-byte diff. The markup is bone-grafting.astro:548-565 element
        for element: head div beside a <dl> of div-wrapped dt/dd rows (the div
        wrapper is valid dl content and is what the source page ships).

        THE WRAPPER IS INSIDE THE TEST. The head renders without rows and the
        rows render without a head — a shape that silently requires both
        halves before drawing either is the bone-grafting #what failure. */
      hasGlossary && (
        <div class="materials-block">
          {hasGlossaryHead && (
            <div class="materials-head">
              {glossaryEyebrowText && <span class="eyebrow">{glossaryEyebrowText}</span>}
              {glossaryHeadingText && <h3>{glossaryHeadingText}</h3>}
              {glossaryBodyText && <p>{glossaryBodyText}</p>}
            </div>
          )}
          {glossaryRows.length > 0 && (
            <dl class="materials-list">
              {glossaryRows.map((row) => (
                <div class="material-row">
                  <dt>
                    {row.tag && <span class="tag">{row.tag}</span>}
                    {row.title && <span class="label">{row.title}</span>}
                  </dt>
                  {row.body && <dd>{row.body}</dd>}
                </div>
              ))}
            </dl>
          )}
        </div>
      )
    }

    <div class="cta-row">
      <div class="cta-buttons">
        {ctaOne && (
          <Button
            variant={isDarkBand ? "light" : "sage"}
            href={ctaOne.href}
            hoverLabel={ctaOne.hover}>{ctaOne.label}</Button>
        )}
        {ctaTwo && (
          <Button
            variant={isDarkBand ? "ghost-light" : "ghost"}
            href={ctaTwo.href}
            hoverLabel={ctaTwo.hover}>{ctaTwo.label}</Button>
        )}
      </div>
      {ctaNoteText && <span class="cta-note">{ctaNoteText}</span>}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ── Band shell ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Lifted from src/styles/pages/clear-aligners.css:228-283 with the
     `.caligners` prefix stripped and the page's local `--sage` / `--ink` / …
     aliases resolved back to the `--vs-*` tokens they alias one-for-one
     (clear-aligners.css:24-42). Rendered values are unchanged.

